module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_refused_round_uploads_no_candidate_and_its_emission_is_lost

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundOnRung, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable, Mitigatable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

data a_refused_round_uploads_no_candidate_and_its_emission_is_lost: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_refused_round_uploads_no_candidate_and_its_emission_is_lost" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundOnRung { rung: Mitigatable },
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "upload the round candidate as evidence under a distinct artifact name when the round refuses, while publishing only on convergence",
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "**a refused round uploads no candidate, so what it would have produced is lost** (INVALID STATE: a generation runs to completion, writes a whole candidate tree into the runner's workspace, and then refuses at a later phase. The step that would have carried that tree out of the runner is conditioned on the phase that refused, so it never runs, and the tree dies with the runner. What survives the job is a verdict line and a list of path NAMES; the bytes those names denote are gone.)",

    "MECHANISM. heal.yml's upload step (actions/upload-artifact, artifact name heal-repair-candidate, if-no-files-found set to error) carries no unconditional execution, so a round that exits non-zero skips it entirely. The publisher heal-publish.yml then downloads by pattern from the triggering run and finds nothing to publish. Two facts are true at the moment of refusal -- the round refused, and the round had already written a complete candidate -- and only the first one survives the job.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVES REVIEW: the workflow reads correctly as a publisher. Publishing a candidate from a refused round WOULD be wrong, so conditioning the upload on success is the right rule for the artifact that gets published. The defect is invisible because it is not a publishing bug at all: it is the absence of a SECOND, evidence-only transport beside the publisher, and an absent step produces no diff for anyone to review.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE (mechanical, at diff grain): for any step that consumes an artifact an earlier step produced, ask what the FAILING path leaves behind. Where the producing step can succeed while the enclosing job fails, and the upload is conditioned on job success, the failure path has no artifact, and every later diagnosis of that failure must re-run the producer at its full cost. A refusal whose every artifact is conditional is a refusal that cannot be inspected, which is what DESIGN section 5 refuses to accept.",

    "SPECIMEN (heal run 41748758669, branch wave/import-only-algebra-heads at dd3c5c1515be, conclusion FAILURE, 2026-11-04 00:41:58Z to 01:19:35Z). The round printed first_generation_equal=false with candidate=target/stage0-regen-candidate -- a complete emitted mirror set -- and then regen-round-cost: refused. The upload step never ran, so heal-publish run 41753277888 (success, 20 seconds, 01:19:39Z to 01:19:59Z) reported Found 0 artifact(s) and Total of 0 artifact(s) downloaded and published nothing. The residue is one log line naming 15 drifted paths; the bytes behind those names did not survive.",

    "HARM, measured. The question the candidate answers -- WHICH BYTES differ, not merely which files -- could not be answered from CI at all. Answering it required a separate ruled host run: build the regenerator at the head (about 10 minutes) and run the generation child alone (about 14 minutes, child-only peak 11.76 GB read from run 41720970373's own cgroup lines), plus a reviewed leg script, in order to obtain one file. The emission the round had ALREADY produced would have answered the same question for the cost of a download.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: Mitigatable. The mitigation exists and works -- an out-of-band leg that rebuilds the emission at the head -- but it is a hand-run script plus a host ruling every single time, not a mechanism in the corpus, so the class recurs at every refused round rather than being discharged once.",

    "CEILING, derived: MechanicallyPreventable. Whether a round refused and what it wrote are both facts the runner already holds at the instant of refusal; carrying the second fact out under a name the publisher never reads needs no new judgement, no new model, and no new capability anywhere else in the system. It is a property of the workflow, which is what makes the rung mechanical rather than structural.",

    "MEASURED AGAIN, FROM THE PUBLISHER SIDE (2026-11-04 21:06Z to 2026-11-05). The class recurs where the consumer is the one left guessing. Thirteen consecutive heal-publish runs FAILED with their Admit step executing, and the last Admit that EXECUTED and passed was run 41909205210 at 20:42:51Z; across 25 earlier heal-publish runs, 23 skipped both Admit and Publish and still reported SUCCESS. The failure path is ordinary: actions/download-artifact logs "Found 0 artifact(s)", then "Total of 0 artifact(s) downloaded", then "Download artifact has finished successfully" (job 126921178053 of run 41929113822, whose trigger 41919581962 was CANCELLED), and every step that could have said so is gated on candidate blob files having landed. So the publisher reported success while admitting it had seen nothing -- and the TRIGGERING RUN'S CONCLUSION, present in every one of those runs, was read by nobody. A green that asserts nothing is this row's defect seen from the far end: the emission is gone, and the consumer cannot even ask which kind of nothing it is looking at.",

    "WHAT IS BUILT NOW, AND THE HALF THAT IS NOT (2026-11-05). The second transport exists in the model: gunbc.heal_workflow opens a one-file round outcome record under runner.temp before the round runs, closes it with `if: always()` carrying how many candidate blob files the round had written, and uploads it under gunbc.heal_publication_boundary heal_round_outcome_artifact_name; gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow fetches it UNGATED and says which kind of nothing arrived, refusing a lost emission (blobs recorded, none delivered), a record the round never closed, and the absence of any record. So THE FACT THAT A ROUND WROTE BLOBS NOW SURVIVES THE ROUND, and a publisher green names which case it is. WHAT REMAINS UNBUILT is the bytes themselves: a refused round's candidate tree still dies with the runner, so the question WHICH BYTES differ still cannot be answered from CI -- only the count and the refusal can. Uploading that tree as EVIDENCE under a distinct name is the unbuilt half, and it is named here rather than remembered, together with why it was not built in this change: the size, retention and exposure of carrying refused bytes out of every failed round were never weighed, and the publisher's question -- which kind of nothing -- is answered by the count without them.",

    "THE CONSTRAINT THAT STILL BINDS THE UNBUILT HALF. The two transports must stay distinct: an evidence artifact the publisher is willing to PUBLISH FROM would let a refused round publish, which is the failure this row exists to prevent, not to enable. The built transport honours that by construction -- publication is still gated on the candidate artifact under its own name and on blob files under theirs, and the record has neither name -- and dag/test/claim/repository_ci_profile_witness the_round_outcome_record_is_never_a_publishable_candidate reddens if a rename or a widened gate ever breaks it.",

    "NEXT TRIGGER, named as a capability: upload the round candidate as EVIDENCE under a distinct artifact name when the round refuses, while publishing only on convergence. The two transports must stay distinct -- an evidence artifact the publisher is willing to read would let a refused round publish, which is the failure this row exists to prevent, not to enable. This row records the class only: the job roster and the workflows are closed to growth without a ruling, so nothing in this change builds the trigger.",

    "BOUNDED AGAINST NEIGHBOURS: [[roster_is_its_own_denominator]] is a denominator defect, where the enumeration IS the population so the instrument reading it cannot notice the population is short; here the population exists, is complete, and is destroyed before anyone can read it, which is a transport defect. [[a_v1_unit_test_red_on_main_runs_in_no_required_lane]] is a fact observed in no required lane: there the evidence persists and nobody runs the check, here the check runs, produces the evidence, and the evidence is deleted. [[a_check_and_its_subject_are_removed_in_one_edit]] is a control and its subject leaving together; here the control is not removed at all -- it is unreachable on exactly the path where it is needed.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.heal_publication_boundary", decl_name: "heal_round_outcome_artifact_name"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.heal_workflow", decl_name: "heal_round_outcome_write_step"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow", decl_name: "heal_publisher_round_outcome_decision_step"),
  ],
}
